  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    The kelp-runner brought word at dawn: three penguins dead in the lower veins beneath the Slew, throats opened in the old way, claws raked across ice to form the kill-mark Zund Gnasharkin used to claim his work. Except Zund Gnasharkin had been dead six moon-cycles, crushed beneath the Shelf Crushing Offense when ☽'Netemia's sorcery brought the ice-ceiling down on both armies at once.
    
    Grul Grulrend didn't need to hear the rest. He knew what left those marks. Grungruk's Claw didn't die with its wielder.
    
    "The penguins say it walks the tunnels by itself," the runner said, mandibles clicking. "Kills anyone who looks at it wrong."
    
    "It doesn't walk." Grul Grulrend pressed his scarred flank against the current and felt the rigid tissue pull tight from dorsal to belly—three grooves carved by an aurora-lance, a wound that predicted his movements to anything watching. Zund used to guard that flank without asking. "It waits."
    
    The runner hesitated. "The Matriarch said—"
    
    "The Matriarch doesn't know what I know." Grul Grulrend turned toward the Berg's shadow, massive against the twilight horizon. "Zund Gnasharkin was my pod-brother. That weapon belongs with the pod."
    
    The pod had given him what they could: supply caches marked in the old way, kelp-coordinates scratched into bone fragments, the names of orca who'd tried before and the depths where their bodies still drifted. Three had reached for Grungruk's Claw after Zund fell. One returned pressure-sick, his echolocation shattered; he couldn't hunt, couldn't navigate, spent his last season swimming in circles until the pod let the cold take him. The other two simply vanished. *The weapon doesn't break cleanly.* The elders said it like a proverb, like something you taught pups to keep them from chasing dangerous prey. Grul Grulrend had watched Zund carry that trident for eight seasons and never once seen it break anyone.
    
    But Zund was dead. And the aurora-crystal against Grul Grulrend's ribs had gone cold three days ago—no warmth, no pulse, no memory of the penguin who'd carried it into the dark. Just pressure. Just the weight of water above and the knowledge that he'd swum past the last courier-station two leagues back.
    
    He left the runner behind and swam toward the Long Dark, and the only thing watching his blind side was whatever gathered at the edge of perception when the season turned.
    
    ---
    
    The first body appeared at the edge of Midway Shelf.
    
    Seventeen skeletons marked the boundary—couriers who'd violated the Flipper Accord, left to freeze as warning and example. Grul Grulrend knew the count because he'd swum past them twice before, always with pod-mates, always with the protection of numbers. Now he drifted alone past the bone-cairn and felt the empty eye-sockets track his movement.
    
    Not the skeletons. Something behind them. Something that used their shapes the way a hunter uses a reef for cover.
    
    He pressed on. The cold deepened. Fire-core light flickered in the distance—the Nightshelf's outer sentries, their bone-sutured technology pulsing with captured heat. Star∴eternal, the pod called it. The fire that never slept.
    
    One of the sentries turned. Grul Grulrend felt the tremor in the water—pressure-depth magic reading the ice, the blood, the weight of anything that moved through the dark. The trident had been forged with pressure-magic. If the sentry's senses reached far enough—
    
    He dove. Hard left, compensating for the rigid flank, scraping against a shelf-edge that tore the tissue over his ribs. The aurora-crystal cracked.
    
    Silence. The sentry's attention passed over him, searching deeper, finding nothing worth the hunt.
    
    Grul Grulrend waited in the lee of an ice-pillar until his blood stopped clouding the water. The crystal was useless now—just milky fragments against his skin, the last warmth of a kill he'd made three moon-cycles ago. He'd kept it because Zund would have asked why he'd thrown it back. Zund always asked. Zund never let the pod forget who they were killing.
    
    He found the next sign three hundred fathoms down, where the ice met volcanic stone in a network of cracks the penguins called service-veins. Someone had carved a warning into the shelf-face in old orca script: *Turn-back-turn. Blood-price-paid. Weapon-drinks.*
    
    The carving was fresh. Five, maybe six days old.
    
    Below it, a harness made of kelp-cord and scavenged penguin leather, still bearing frost. He recognized the weave—Krath-pod, cousins to his own bloodline, good raiders who knew better than to swim alone into Nightshelf territory. The harness was empty. No body. No blood-trail. Just three parallel scratches in the ice, carved so deep the stone beneath showed through.
    
    Grul Grulrend touched them with his flipper and felt the cold travel up his arm into his chest. Not ice-cold. Something older. The kind of cold that came from the deep places where the dead god supposedly drifted, waiting for enough blood to wake it.
    
    *Blood remembers what ice forgets.* Another proverb. Another thing the elders said to justify the old ways, the blood-rituals, the obligation to recover what the dead left behind. *The pod doesn't abandon the broken—they use them differently.* A maimed hunter could still swim. A dead pod-brother's weapon could still sing.
    
    "You're three days behind."
    
    He spun—too slow on his rigid side, predictable, vulnerable. The penguin floated twenty feet up the current, half-hidden by kelp-shadow, flippers wrapped in charcoal-cloth that meant Nightshelf tunneler, high-rank. The firelight from her harness reflected off ice in fractures that made her look like she was burning from the inside.
    
    ☽'Netemia Glow-bringer. He knew her from the kill-reports. Zund's greatest enemy. The one who brought the ceiling down.
    
    "I tracked the weapon for four days," she said. Her voice carried the strange echo penguins got when they'd been underground too long, like she was speaking from inside a cave. Her flippers shook with a tremor that never stopped—fire-memory from holding cores until her bones remembered the heat. "Lost two guild-mates. It's in the Slew now, in the deep altars where the Wake-Singers performed the old rites. It killed three Watchers yesterday. They thought fire-wards would hold it."
    
    "Why are you telling me this?"
    
    "Because you're going after it anyway, and I'd rather you die where I can watch." The scar on her jaw was visible even in the dim light: Zund's mark, the wound she'd refused to let healers smooth. "That weapon killed seventeen of my colony. I want it destroyed. You want it claimed. We can't both get what we want."
    
    "So we race."
    
    "So we race." She tilted her head in the way penguins did when they were reading aurora-patterns—or calculating kill-angles. "One courtesy, raider: the weapon isn't waiting quietly anymore. Something's changed. The fracture spreads. And whatever it remembers of Zund Gnasharkinit remembers the hunger, not the sense."
    
    She dove before he could answer, flippers cutting water with the kind of precision that came from Star∴eternal training—the bone-fire art that let penguins sense through ice. The light from her harness faded down into the Berg's throat, leaving him with the scratches and the cold and the certainty that she was right.
    
    The path to the Slew curved through territory without courier-protection. Grul Grulrend had memorized the route from a dying informant—a penguin trader who'd sold the wrong secrets to the wrong pod. The trader had spoken of a side-channel, a passage the Wake-Singers used during the Shattered-Spire Assault Desecrated ground. Corrupted ice. The kind of place the sentries avoided because even their fire-sutured senses couldn't read what the ice remembered.
    
    He found the entrance an hour later. A crack in the shelf-wall, barely wide enough for his bulk, rimmed with discoloration that pulsed faintly when he approached.
    
    Grul Grulrend squeezed through, and the ice began to whisper.
    
    ---
    
    The Thewsed Slew opened around him like a wound.
    
    Fire-core light guttered in forge-channels that hadn't been worked in seasons—not the clean burn of active smithing, but the char-stench of something that had died badly and refused to stop smoldering. The main chamber's eastern wall bore claw-marks deep enough to fit a flipper; orca gouges from the Incursion, patched with salvage-stone that didn't match. Never would. Altar-surfaces in the lower levels still carried dark-stains from Wake-Singer rituals. The Nightshelf had cleansed this place. Cleansing wasn't the same as forgetting.
    
    And the ice remembered everything.
    
    Grul Grulrend felt it the moment he crossed the threshold—impressions pressing against his echolocation, fragments of violence that the corrupted stone had absorbed and couldn't release. He heard echoes of penguin screams. Orca kill-songs. The grinding collapse of ice on bone. The Shelf Crushing Offense had ended here, or near enough; the sorcerers who'd unmade Zund had worked their magic in the veins below, and the temple had caught the resonance like a tuning fork catching sound.
    
    He saw the bodies first.
    
    Four penguins, arranged in a half-circle facing the northern altar, throats opened with the precision Zund Gnasharkin used when he wanted the kill to mean something. Their harnesses still burned with fire-cores. No scavenger had touched them. Even the ice-mites avoided the corpses.
    
    He swam deeper. Past the forge-platforms. Past the carved mark on the northern support-beam—three names and a date, scratched with something sharp. Past the altar where Wake-Singer blood had pooled before the cleansing. The fire-cores pulsed hotter as he descended, and the whispers grew louder, and then—
    
    There.
    
    Grungruk's Claw rested in a depression of corrupted ice at the altar's base, three prongs angled upward like a reef-creature waiting to strike. The whale-bone had darkened by pressure-magic until it resembled charred driftwood. Deep-sea coral bound the shaft in a grip stained with penguin blood that no amount of washing would touch. The central prong—the killing prong—bore a hairline fracture that had appeared the moment Zund drove it through the ice-ceiling, and the crack had spread since then. Slowly. Visibly. Like scar-tissue creeping across healthy bone.
    
    The fracture wept something that looked like water but moved wrong—too thick, too slow.
    
    The weapon was bleeding.
    
    Grul Grulrend floated above it and did not reach down.
    
    Six moon-cycles of swimming. Three pod-mates dead or broken. The weapon that had sung when it tasted blood, that had carried Zund's rage into the heart of the Nightshelf's defenses, that had cracked open the Shelf Crushing Offense and brought the ceiling down on everyone beneath—
    
    It wasn't dormant. It was waiting. The difference mattered.
    
    "Don't touch it."
    
    ☽'Netemia stepped from the shadows near the eastern wall. Her flippers still shook with their constant fire-tremor, and her charcoal wraps couldn't hide the texture of scar-tissue beneath. She'd held fire-cores until her bones remembered the heat. She'd fought the Incursion and carried costs. She looked like she'd been here for hours—like she'd beaten him through passages she knew better, through a temple her people had built and bled for.
    
    She knew exactly what the trident had done to the defenders of this place.
    
    "I tried," she said. Her voice was flat, exhausted, the voice of someone who'd been waiting too long for something inevitable. "It won't let go of the ice. It's waiting for someone who knew him."
    
    "It was Zund's."
    
    "It was." She didn't move closer, didn't draw the blade sheathed at her side. "And Zund is dead. We recovered the trident from his grip after the collapse. It took four of us. Two didn't survive the week."
    
    Grul Grulrend stared at the weapon. The fracture pulsed in the fire-core light—or maybe that was the corruption in the ice, the impressions of violence pressing against the stone.
    
    "The pod doesn't abandon what the dead leave behind," he said. "We use them differently."
    
    "Then use it." ☽'Netemia's tremoring flipper gestured toward the trident. "Pick it up. Carry it back to your pod. See what it does to you."
    
    She wasn't guarding it. She was waiting to see what happened when he tried.
    
    ---
    
    The ice remembered Zund.
    
    Grul Grulrend felt it the moment his fin brushed the corrupted ice around the trident—not a vision, not a voice, but an impression so sharp it cut. Zund gripping the shaft. Zund driving it upward into the ceiling. Zund pinned beneath the collapse, still trying to thrust deeper, still trying to crack the shelf open one last time even as the sorcery unmade him breath by breath.
    
    He didn't call for rescue. At the end, he just kept driving the point deeper.
    
    The weapon had watched him die. The weapon had drunk his last rage. It had learned what it meant to want something badly enough to die for it, and now it carried that wanting like a sickness in the bone.
    
    The fracture spread another hairline's width as Grul Grulrend's fin closed around the shaft.
    
    Pain. Not physical—or not only physical. Something in the coral grip recognized him, recognized the shape of loss he carried, the seasons of guarding Zund's blind side and being guarded in return. The trident had known a pod-bond before. It remembered how that bond felt when it broke.
    
    Cold flooded his chest. The Slew vanished. He was in the Shelf Crushing Offense again, ice collapsing, Zund's voice screaming the old war-songs, the ceiling coming down in sheets of crystal and stone. He felt the weapon drive upward, felt it crack, felt something vast and hungry wake in the deep below and taste the blood-offering and find it *insufficient*—
    
    He was back in the Slew, gasping, the trident in his grip.
    
    It was heavier than stone. The crack wept its thick not-water down his arm, cold enough to burn.
    
    ☽'Netemia watched from the shadows. Her tremoring flippers were still. Waiting.
    
    "He didn't call for rescue," Grul Grulrend said. His voice came out strange—too low, thick with something that wasn't water. "Did he?"
    
    "No." Her jaw-scar caught the light. "He just kept driving the point deeper. Trying to finish what your pod started."
    
    *Blood remembers what ice forgets.* The trident remembered. The corruption in the ice remembered. And Grul Grulrend with his rigid flank and his dead pod-brother's weapon in his grip, remembered what Zund had always taught him: the pod doesn't abandon the broken.
    
    But this was broken in a way that couldn't be used differently. This was a fracture that spread, that killed everyone who reached for it, that carried Zund's dying rage like a prayer to a dead god's hunger. Not a weapon anymore. A promise Zund Gnasharkin had made—and promises don't die just because the one who made them does.
    
    "It's not finished," Grul Grulrend said. "Zund drove it into the vault trying to crack the Berg's heart. The weapon broke instead. Now it wants to finish what he started."
    
    "Then it has to be held." ☽'Netemia's voice was quiet. "There's a place deeper than the Slew. Where the Berg's ice meets the first stone, before the volcanic veins corrupted it. Memory-ice, the elders call it. It remembers everything that ever happened here. If you drove the weapon deep enough—"
    
    "The ice would remember it. Hold it. Forever."
    
    "Or until the Berg melts. Which is the same as forever."
    
    Grul Grulrend felt the weapon's weight in his grip, felt the cold spreading up his arm, felt the crack widen. Felt Zund's presence in the bone and coral, still hunting, still hungry, still trying to crack the Berg's heart even though his body was crushed beneath tons of ice.
    
    "He would have hated this," Grul Grulrend said. "Being trapped. Being held."
    
    "Yes."
    
    "But he would have hated failing more."
    
    He let the trident settle back into the corrupted ice. Not because he didn't understand—but because he did. The pod had sent him to bring it back. He could tell them where it rested. Tell them what it cost. Let them send someone else if they wanted it badly enough.
    
    But no one would come who understood. That was the cruelty of it. The ones strong enough to lift the weapon wouldn't know to put it down. The ones wise enough to leave it would never reach for it at all. And Grul Grulrendscarred flank, dead crystal, empty grip—was the only one who'd done both.
    
    He turned toward the Slew's deepest shaft, where the fire-cores couldn't reach and the ice remembered the Berg's first age. Then he stopped. Looked back at the trident. At the fracture weeping its slow blood. At ☽'Netemia, standing vigil in the ruin of a temple his people had tried to break open and hers had tried to hold shut.
    
    "The pod won't stop," he said. "Someone will come again."
    
    "I know." She moved back toward her shadows, toward whatever watch she kept in this desecrated place. "Blood remembers. That's the problem."
    
    Grul Grulrend swam out through the corrupted passage, past the fire-core sentries, past the bone-cairn at Midway Shelf, into the Long Dark where the watchers gathered at the edge of perception. The trident stayed behind, waiting in its nest of broken ice.
    
    The fracture would keep spreading. Slowly. Visibly. Like scar-tissue that didn't know the wound was done.
    
    And when the next orca came—the next pod-mate with an obligation they couldn't abandon—Grungruk's Claw would be ready. It doesn't walk. It waits.
    
    The ice remembers. That has to be enough.
  CANONICAL_CHRONICLE
  annotation "note_1771588640825_0", anchor: "mandibles clicking", text: "Penguins do not have mandibles. The term is occasionally misapplied to beak articulation in accounts transcribed from orca informants, who use their own anatomical vocabulary indiscriminately. The chronicler should have corrected on transcription.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771588640825_1", anchor: "☽'Netemia's sorcery brought the ice-ceiling down on both armies at once", text: "One sorcerer in the opening. 'The sorcerers who'd unmade Zund' three pages later. The plural is correct. Foundation ice preserves Nightshelf bioluminescent pulse-codes running through the eastern tunnels throughout the engagement — coordination signals for an operation involving at minimum three intelligence operatives managing evacuation and feint positions. The chronicler collapses this into a lone heroic act at the opening, then inadvertently restores the truth when the narrative requires tactical detail. I documented this pattern in my annotation on ☽'Netemia's entry. It has not been corrected because the simpler version is more useful to everyone except the dead.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771588640825_2", anchor: "Blood remembers what ice forgets", text: "The ice does not forget. That is its defining property. The orca proverb is revealing not for what it claims but for what it assumes: that ice is inert, a medium rather than a witness. The Berg's ice would disagree.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771588640825_3", anchor: "killed seventeen of my colony", text: "Seventeen. The same number as the courier skeletons at Midway Shelf, referenced two pages earlier. Coincidence, or a chronicler with a fondness for structural echo. I have learned not to trust any figure that appears twice in the same account.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771588640825_4", anchor: "an impression so sharp it cut", text: "This chronicle is set during the Long Dark, and that matters more than the narrative acknowledges. When Grul touches the trident and experiences Zund's death not as recollection but as immersion — present-tense, bodily, sharp enough to cut — the thinned boundary between the living world and ice-memory is doing its work. During the Long Dark the veil grows permeable. Visions come unbidden. The dead speak more clearly. Every artifact is more volatile when the season turns, every impression closer to the surface. The trident's corruption is real, but the Long Dark amplifies it. The chronicler treats the timing as atmospheric. It is mechanical.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771588640825_5", anchor: "the watchers gathered at the edge of perception", text: "Left unelaborated, as though naming them further would give them purchase. An orca — outside both penguin theological traditions — swims into their presence without comment. The deep ice contains impressions from beings that predate penguin sapience. Something was here first. It watches during the Long Dark because that is when it can.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771588640825_6", anchor: "Where the Berg's ice meets the first stone, before the volcanic veins corrupted it", text: "The orca elder tradition names it memory-ice. We call it the Foundation Depths. If they had driven the weapon deep enough, I would have catalogued it eventually.", type: :tangent, display: :popout
